      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "The great thing about Steam is that it has already supported regional pricing for years. There's a website called SteamDB.info where you can view the prices of games in different regions. The majority of the currencies that Steam supports tend to be discounted when compared with the U.S. Dollar.\nI'm from South Africa and our currency, the 'South African Rand' (ZAR), has an unfavourable exchange rate with the U.S. Dollar. However, the ZAR price of most games on Steam is lower than a direct USD to ZAR conversion, which helps a lot with affordability. The consequence for developers and publishers is that they receive less money from South African customers but it's better than getting no money at all due to customers deciding that they can't afford it.\nLooking through a variety of examples on SteamDB, games are discounted for South Africans by anywhere between -10% to -70%. Certain big, greedy publishers charge us more (up to +10%) but I don't tend to buy those games.\nI'd really like to get a yearly subscription to VRC+ but, at the current exchange rate, this comes to about R1,500. I've hovered my pointer over the buy button a couple of times but I just feel like I'd be a fool for spending that amount of money for what VRC+ provides. \nI think that, for South Africans, the yearly subscription should be at most R999. That would be a -33% discount, which is pretty middle-of-the-road and would be good enough to prevent me from feeling like an idiot for buying it.\n(I should add that Valve's suggested pricing for the $9.99 price category is R79 and, for the $99.99 price category, it's actually just R549.)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
